  doi = {10.1016/j.mhp.2018.10.003},
  urldate = {2023-12-01},
  abstract = {Substance use disorders and personality disorders do very commonly co-occur. A substance use disorder can be diagnosed in approximately 50\% of patients suffering from a personality disorder, depending on sample and setting. In drug use disorders comorbid personality disorders seem to occur more often than in alcohol use disorder. A particularly frequent association has been found between substance use disorder and antisocial or borderline personality disorder. Generally, these comorbidities are characterized by problems due to severe addiction and unfavorable clinical courses. There is growing evidence for the need of specialized therapies for patients with personality disorder and comorbid substance use disorder. Thus, optimizing treatments seems of particular importance. In most cases psychotherapy is the treatment of choice for patients with personality disorders. In acute crisis and other comorbid psychiatric disorders, such as psychosis and depression, pharmacotherapy is useful. Mainly these three different evidence-based psychotherapies have been scientifically assessed for comorbid patients: dual-focused schema therapy, dialectical behavior therapy and dynamic deconstructive psychotherapy. No controlled trials of specialized pharmacotherapy for patients with personality disorder and co-occurring substance use disorder have been conducted. In conclusion, therapeutic approaches for the two disorders should comprise combined treatment methods. To improve the specific treatment options for patients with substance use disorder and personality disorder further research is needed.},
  keywords = {Addiction,Dual disorders,Personality disorder,Pharmacotherapy,Psychotherapy,Substance use disorder},
